      {
        "tests": "test_checkpoints: 13 distinct tests on Windows Python 3.14 and 11 on 3.15; all passed on Windows Python 3.11 and on macOS/Linux",
        "symptom": "Valid checkpoints were listed as unavailable without a revision, so deletion tests raised KeyError; which tests failed differed between jobs.",
        "cause": "On Windows, CPython 3.12+ os.stat/os.lstat copy the creation time into st_ctime (win32_xstat), while os.fstat reports the change time (_Py_fstat_noraise). The checkpoint identity included st_ctime_ns and compared a path stat with fstat, so it failed whenever a file's change time differed from its creation time.",
        "product_impact": "Fail-closed: nothing was wrongly deleted, but on Windows with Python 3.12+ checkpoints could not be listed or deleted reliably.",
        "fix": "identity() leaves st_ctime_ns out on Windows only. The file ID, size, mtime and the content hash in the revision still detect a replaced or changed file; other platforms keep st_ctime_ns."
      },
      {
        "tests": "test_process_review.test_cli_default_does_not_disclose_private_details on all three Windows Pythons",
        "cause": "The test searched JSON output for the raw temporary path, but JSON doubles Windows backslashes; the matching absence checks (CLI default and anonymous MCP view) therefore passed vacuously on Windows.",
        "fix": "Tests look for the path as JSON writes it. Product code unchanged."
      }
    ],
